</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>The third pipeline with LoReB Distillation</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show the experimental results of the application of the encoder of LoReB pipeline.
It was fine-tuned on approximately <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="110,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>110,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">110,000</annotation></semantics></math> samples, half of which were pairs of English-Bambara and the other half of French-Bambara. The datasets used were Dokotoro and a randomly generated subset of sentences from NLLB. The encoder was fine-tuned for 100 epochs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.1 </span>Feature extraction: comparison between models</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fine-tuning process of the LaBSE model involves different stages and settings, each characterized by varying degrees of model adaptation and performance using only the embeddings. For instance, Figure <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7.3.1 Feature extraction: comparison between models ‣ 7.3 The third pipeline with LoReB Distillation ‣ 7 Experiments and results ‣ A comparison of pipelines for the translation of a low resource language based on transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the embedding space (originally with 768 dimensions) of Dokotoro sentences in English, French and Bambara. The figure shows the result of a PCA decomposition of the embeddings, projected along the first two principal components, only. Since the projection is an approximation, the picture could reveal some kind of disalignment. However, as shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7.3.1 Feature extraction: comparison between models ‣ 7.3 The third pipeline with LoReB Distillation ‣ 7 Experiments and results ‣ A comparison of pipelines for the translation of a low resource language based on transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> high-resource languages (blue and green) cluster more closely than the low-resource language (yellow), indicating stronger semantic alignment for high-resource languages. After the training epochs, however, the cluster of Bambara embeddings shows the clear tendency to converge into a more unified embedding space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12514v1/LoReB.svg" id="S7.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/343;" width="429" height="343"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>LaBSE embeddings after LoReB fine-tuning
</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S7.F3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:1.0pt;"></p></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.2 </span>Distributions of distances</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 7.3.2 Distributions of distances ‣ 7.3 The third pipeline with LoReB Distillation ‣ 7 Experiments and results ‣ A comparison of pipelines for the translation of a low resource language based on transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the distribution of cosine similarity scores between the embeddings of the source language and the embeddings of the target language after the encoding phase of LoReB. It has a roughly normal shape, centred around <math id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.65-0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.65</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.65-0.7</annotation></semantics></math>. This indicates that most sentence pairs embeddings are moderately to highly similar, suggesting a generally strong semantic alignment. A smaller number of pairs show very high or very low similarity, reflecting occasional near-duplicates or mismatches. Overall, the distribution supports the presence of meaningful correspondence between the encoded sentences.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12514v1/distrib_cosine.svg" id="S7.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/257;" width="429" height="257"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Distribution of cosine similarity on the embeddings of source and target sentences produced by the encoder in LoReB</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S7.F4.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;"></p></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.3 </span>LoReB translation</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7.3.3 LoReB translation ‣ 7.3 The third pipeline with LoReB Distillation ‣ 7 Experiments and results ‣ A comparison of pipelines for the translation of a low resource language based on transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the progress of training of the decoder T5 needed to generate a translation from a source sentence (French) into a target one (Bambara).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12514v1/plot_loss_T5.svg" id="S7.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The training progress in LoReB: (Left) the convergence in loss in training and evaluation sets; (Right) the increase in average BLEU score
</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S7.F5.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;"></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7.3.3 LoReB translation ‣ 7.3 The third pipeline with LoReB Distillation ‣ 7 Experiments and results ‣ A comparison of pipelines for the translation of a low resource language based on transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows a consistent decrease in average loss and a corresponding increase in BLEU score over epochs, indicating effective learning and improved alignment with the reference targets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental results of LoReB in the translation from French to Bambara on the benchmarks test sets are shown in Table <a href="#S7.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 7.3.3 LoReB translation ‣ 7.3 The third pipeline with LoReB Distillation ‣ 7 Experiments and results ‣ A comparison of pipelines for the translation of a low resource language based on transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Translation performance (BLEU and chrF) of T5 in LoReB on French-to-Bambara</figcaption>
<table id="S7.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S7.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S7.T6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S7.T6.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BLEU [%]</span></td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S7.T6.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">chrF</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Yiri test set</td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">13.21</td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">34.15</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Bayelemabaga</td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.6</td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">27.39</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">MAFAND-MT</td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1.12</td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">33.16</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T6.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T6.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">FLORES+</td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">1.20</td>
<td id="S7.T6.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">28.17</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From the experimental results of the BLEU and chrF scores of the three pipelines, it seems that FLORES+ allows less promising results than the other datasets. Similar results have also been observed in other works on NMT for LRLs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silva et al.(2024)Silva, Srivastava, Moteu Ngoli, Röder, Moussallem, and Ngonga Ngomo</a>]</cite> (although not with Bambara and on FLORES-200, which is similar enough to FLORES+ but simpler, because it focuses on less nuanced contexts).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally in Table <a href="#S7.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 7.3.3 LoReB translation ‣ 7.3 The third pipeline with LoReB Distillation ‣ 7 Experiments and results ‣ A comparison of pipelines for the translation of a low resource language based on transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> we compare the amount of computational resources employed for the three pipelines. For time and resource constraints we had to use different HPC architectures in which T4 support for training is limited, L4 discrete and A100 excellent.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Amount of computational resources in training</figcaption>
